Based on my review of the cross-common-dir auto-adopt claim mechanism (the double-adopt fix in `bcced022`), I found one real gap in the mutual-exclusion guarantee.

**Finding:** In `adoptFromExternalSessionStore` (`cmd/entire/cli/session_adopt.go:179`), the `AdoptClaim` check that prevents double-adopting a session is gated on `opts.DeferSourceRetire`:

```go
if opts.DeferSourceRetire && adoptClaimBlocks(sourceState.AdoptClaim, targetCommonDir) {
    return &sourceClaimedError{...}
}
```

This means only a second *auto-adopt* (which always sets `DeferSourceRetire: true`) respects an existing claim. A manual `entire session adopt --from <source> --force` (which leaves `DeferSourceRetire` false) does **not** check `AdoptClaim` at all — it will happily load the source, build a new adopted copy, and immediately tombstone the source, even while another target has it claimed and non-destructively registered via auto-adopt but not yet finalized in post-commit. That races two live copies of the same session into two different repos — exactly the corruption this commit set out to prevent — because the guard only defends auto-adopt against auto-adopt, not manual adopt against an in-flight auto-adopt claim.
